		{
			"id":"250401e6c722d2d1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"### 1734)\n\nAlso known as **\"Hogarth’s Act**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-11705,
			"y":3395,
			"width":520,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"94da80804b693f8a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"old man yelling at the clouds energy\n?? add meme",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4530,
			"y":733,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"f3f817ca4d964d3a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"how was it received",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4350,
			"y":620,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"92aa118063569517",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🗝️ Any source in particular?\n---\nHis writing at the time, but if you want something precise, check out Daniel Defoe's The Review, Vol. VI, No. 101 on November 10, 1709.\nYou can find a few extracts around that time from the man himself by searching online.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4085,
			"y":317,
			"width":480,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"e302cb83277536b7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🧱 Where can I read the primary source?\n---\nAlthough the content of old periodicals are in the public domain by now, only rare digitized extracts are available to the public.\nMost of them are behind the paywall of private publishers like <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/20779451\" target=\"_blank\">JSTOR</a> or <a href=\"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/mono/10.4324/9781003576228/defoe-review-1704-13-volume-6-1709-10-part-john-mcveagh\" target=\"_blank\">Taylor & Francis</a>.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3960,
			"y":567,
			"width":340,
			"height":226
		},
		{
			"id":"3eecde0cef5e19f0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"to promote the company in pamphlets and a newspaper he edited- he was their spin doctor.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5650,
			"y":257,
			"width":492,
			"height":166
		},
		{
			"id":"c6af20895de1c73a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What was it before",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5092,
			"y":583,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"6bf4d15935fded99",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏴‍☠️ Around 1709, Daniel Defoe's \"Review\" turned its attention to the question of piracy\n---\nNo, not the one in the sea.\nUpset about his financial loss of opportunity cost in his previous experiences, he started claiming a bold new idea: a BOOK should be the AUTHOR’s Property.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4905,
			"y":123,
			"width":390,
			"height":300,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"6d889bbbf0bab92e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ 1709 you say?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4880,
			"y":117,
			"width":681,
			"height":229
		},
		{
			"id":"bad180016d3832e4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H_aOHpn_vqQ\njohn locke\nanne statute\nHogarth act 1734\n\n1. The Stationers' Company — after failing for 15 years to restore their monopoly, they pivoted to arguing \"for authors\" in 1709\n\nDaniel Defoe — published the demand in December 1709 in his \"A Review,\" but he was working for the Tory government at the time\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worshipful_Company_of_Stationers_and_Newspaper_Makers\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6430,
			"y":504,
			"width":1020,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"cc521d00f1380c50",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"whats this list edward darrel, charles harper, robert andrews, freeman collins, samuel roycroft, john baskett, thomas hodgkin, william phillips\n\nThis list consists of notable **English printers and booksellers**, specifically those active during the late 17th and early 18th centuries.\n\nWhile they may not all be household names today, many of them held the prestigious title of **King’s Printer** or were key figures in the **Stationers' Company** in London. This group was responsible for producing official government documents, law books, and the earliest \"mass-market\" literature in England.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6170,
			"y":77,
			"width":460,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"b7564f178fc0ff53",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Defoe is known to have used at least 198 [pen names](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pen_name)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5890,
			"y":417,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"deaf19e215f1f4a6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Around 1709, Daniel Defoe began working for the Royal African Company.\n---\nH",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5548,
			"y":-73,
			"width":390,
			"height":300,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"46ad0b6bb383e724",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"et me summarize this for the user.\nDaniel Defoe's Worst Colonial Sins:\n1. Royal Africa Company agent (1690s) — Directly managed slave trade operations on the Gold Coast. Profit from human trafficking.\n2. Colonial propaganda — Wrote pamphlets defending/ promoting English colonization. UBC thesis titled \"Daniel Defoe as a colonial propagandist\" covers this.\n3. Advocated Transportation Act (1718) — Argued for shipping convicts to colonies as forced labor. His novels Colonel Jack and Moll Flanders romanticize this — criminals \"find salvation\" through colonial transportation.\n4. Pro-slavery rhetoric in novels — His works portray slavery and colonial labor as opportunities for redemption, not exploitation. \"Grateful slaves\" trope.\n5. Profit from forced labor systems — He was personally invested in the colonial economy: trade, transportation, plantation-linked commerce.\nThe irony: The man who wrote \"author protection\" in 1709 was profiting from the most brutal forced labor system in history. His \"property\" argument for books mirrors how slave-owners defined \"property\" in human beings.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7208,
			"y":213,
			"width":660,
			"height":520,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"26fd431168a03ad5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"history of ownership ?? legal document about slave ownership ?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6690,
			"y":-143,
			"width":260,
			"height":220,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"d674c3e21ca88716","type":"text","text":"⭐ The Life of Daniel Defoe\n---\nIn a very condensed format.","x":-3485,"y":2420,"width":1260,"height":945,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"title_aside2","type":"text","text":"Though I guess a lot of them were white men.\n---\nSorry, its mostly their story.\n  But it doesn't have to be about them.","x":-780,"y":4120,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{
			"id":"129c1ea38464bd60",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I know...\n---\nIt's probably more than they deserve.\nIn fact this story is precisely about them getting more than one deserves.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-540,
			"y":4420,
			"width":260,
			"height":220,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{"id":"49209c0bf5486f14","type":"text","text":"I can't help but wonder if this psychological bias in stories reinforces a hyper-individualistic society.\n---","x":-1280,"y":4140,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d0c1f86e10f949da","type":"text","text":"Robinson Crusoe?\n---\nRobinson Crusoe is an English adventure novel by Daniel Defoe, first published on 25 April 1719.\nMost people are familiar with its narrative of a sailor shipwrecked on a wild island.\nBut few people even know the story of the story.","x":-2320,"y":4530,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{
			"id":"5a13efbbc9929464",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"True?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1937,
			"y":4584,
			"width":235,
			"height":93,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"⭐ The true story of the story of Robinson Crusoe\n---","x":-1937,"y":2815,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6a342d3d556dcaa3","type":"text","text":"What do you mean, the story of the story?\n---\nI mean the story of how the book came to be, and the story of its author.","x":-2320,"y":3500,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"title_aside","type":"text","text":"The story of a white man?\n---\nAlas, that is where we must start.\n  Humans do love their stories to be character-centered.\n  But fear not, you will see many characters if you press the right buttons.","x":-1280,"y":3840,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"6"},
