		},
		{"id":"942c917146bc7e21","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n4V2N5yogIE","x":-1517,"y":7886,"width":600,"height":360},
		{
			"id":"e1ef83e94426eeac",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"it is widely credited for inventing realism",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1517,
			"y":7976,
			"width":260,
			"height":150,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"732c607729abf65a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Authenticity",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1087,
			"y":8246,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"99af32b09b97fb14",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The history of PR is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-987,
			"y":8426,
			"width":260,
			"height":105,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"b88844f76bf4c415",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 and thats the end of robinson crusoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1767,
			"y":6577,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"f919133328a5e0d3","type":"text","text":"It was a groundbreaking book\n---\n\nfirst book starting with \"I\"","x":-1657,"y":7061,"width":800,"height":620,"color":"1"},
		{
			"id":"3c9c67e48e96f8d1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"the first modern bestseller ??? ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2192,
			"y":8225,
			"width":630,
			"height":300,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ce2b2e6376144e12",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/55538/55538-h/55538-h.htm\nripoff of real book",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2232,
			"y":8593,
			"width":640,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"ced5ceafeac6ea52",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Where did the middle class money come from?\n- Merchants & traders — Domestic and international trade, East India Company, colonial trade\n- Shopkeepers & retailers — Fixed-price shops replacing markets, selling manufactured goods\n- Professionals — Lawyers, doctors, clergymen, architects, surveyors\n- Bankers & financiers — The City of London, early credit systems\n- Artisans & master craftsmen — Skilled workers, especially in growing manufacturing towns\n- Sea captains & ship owners — Maritime commerce\n- Government officials — Growing bureaucracy\n- Industrialists — Early mining, manufacturing (textiles in Lancashire, iron in Midlands)\nWhere did they live?\n- London (biggest market for books)\n- Port cities: Bristol, Liverpool, Newcastle, Hull\n- Growing market towns\n- Manufacturing centers: Birmingham, Manchester (early)\nWhat was their money worth?\n- Laborer: ~1-2 shillings/week\n- Skilled craftsman: ~2-5 shillings/week\n- Merchant/professional: ~10-50+ shillings/week\nA 3-6 shilling book was affordable for the top 10-20% of society — not the masses, but the emerging commercial/professional class that had:\n- Disposable income (after food, housing)\n- Leisure time (not working from dawn to dusk)\n- Aspiration (self-improvement, \"polite\" culture)\n- Social anxiety (wanting to distinguish from \"lower\" classes)\nThey wanted respectable entertainment — novels presented as \"true accounts\" fit perfectly. It was improving as well as entertaining.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1033,
			"y":7476,
			"width":480,
			"height":340,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"339230b270ddd7cc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The prosperity came from:\n1. Colonial/Atlantic Trade\n- East India Company — spices, textiles, tea, porcelain\n- Atlantic triangle — sugar, tobacco, cotton, rum, slaves\n- The slave trade itself — major profits feeding back to London, Bristol, Liverpool\n2. Domestic Manufacturing\n- Textiles — wool from Yorkshire, cotton emerging in Lancashire\n- Iron — Birmingham, Sheffield (weapons, tools)\n- Coal — Newcastle, powering everything\n3. Enclosure of Common Land\n- Landed g enclosing common fields → evicting peasants\n- Those displaced became workers, or migrated\n- Enclosure made landowners richer, created labor pool\n4. Financial Revolution\n- Bank of England (1694) — credit, national debt, paper money\n- Joint-stock companies — speculation, bubbles\n- Lloyd's — insurance for ships\n5. War Economy\n- Government contracts for army/navy supplies\n- Debt created financial instruments\n- Some made fortunes from supplying wars\n---\nThe dirty secret:\nThe middle class prosperity that let them buy Robinson Crusoe was directly connected to:\n- Slave trade profits (London merchants profited from the triangular trade)\n- Colonial extraction (sugar, tobacco plantations)\n- Enclosure dispossession (peasants pushed off land → labor pool)\n- War-profiteering (contracts, speculation)\nSo the very people reading Defoe's \"author protection\" rhetoric were often profiting from the actual extraction — of labor, land, and colonial subjects.\nThat connection is exactly what you're building into the story, right?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1033,
			"y":7956,
			"width":700,
			"height":240,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"5f23c6534927a79d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"a lot of people thought it was real, It created sort of a reality crisis, you might say.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1113,
			"y":8562,
			"width":520,
			"height":261,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"f12ac0f6ca7ff345",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"defoe would make a habit of this lmao moll flanders, colonel jack,roxana",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1243,
			"y":6676,
			"width":260,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"8e61922d45904866",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Its first edition sold a couple thousand copies, it was an immediate success that created a new reading audience among the middle class\n\n- Robinson Crusoe ≈ Gulliver's Travels — both immediate bestsellers, ~1,000 first edition, sold out fast\n- Pamela was bigger — literally created \"first edition fever,\" queues at bookshops, first novel to have modern \"bestseller\" culture\n- The Pilgrim's Progress had longest tail — printed continuously for 200 years, tens of thousands of copies over decades\nThe pattern: Defoe and Swift (1720s) created the commercial novel. Richardson (1740s) made it mass-culture. Pamela was the true breakthrough in terms of \"everyone's talking about it\" cultural moment.\nThe middle class was the market* — they had money (3-6 shillings), leisure to read, and hunger for \"true stories\" (the authenticity marketing Defoe pioneered).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1243,
			"y":6976,
			"width":540,
			"height":400,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"72d5074d19b63713","type":"text","text":"You forgot the part at the bottom...","x":393,"y":5948,"width":300,"height":80,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"ed26200129d229b8","type":"text","text":"👥 Printed for W. Taylor","x":343,"y":6088,"width":230,"height":100,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"99677d0088771e0d","type":"text","text":"📍 at the Shop in Pater-Noster-Row","x":613,"y":6088,"width":200,"height":120,"color":"0"},
		{
			"id":"a2b8cce11cba48cf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"robinson crusoe is the first book written by a character",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-647,
			"y":6693,
			"width":400,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"abbb1b30cf4e9217",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"also first appeal to authenticity, and first appropriation of another persons story alexander selkirk",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-647,
			"y":6901,
			"width":260,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"cd4e0a6539919426",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"is it public domain ???",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-112,
			"y":6533,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"15e35a10feb5147c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"true\ntheres a lot to unpack here\n\nclickbait etc rework with the notes",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1087,
			"y":6180,
			"width":400,
			"height":340,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"98fa05400360f86f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"- John Bunyan (1678) The Pilgrim's Progress — First major English allegorical novel in first-person, uses \"dream vision\" frame as marketing hook\n- Richard Head (1665-71) The English Rogue — First English picaresque novel in first-person, serialized rogue's memoir based on criminal subculture\n- Daniel Defoe (1719) Robinson Crusoe — Genre-defining fictional autobiography, \"Written by Himself\" marketing trick, based loosely on Alexander Selkirk but never credited\n- Daniel Defoe (1722) Moll Flanders — First-person novel about female criminal life, \"Written from the instance\" framing, researched criminal culture\n- Daniel Defoe (1722) Colonel Jack — Soldier's memoir format, orphan-to-criminal narrative arc, same authenticity marketing formula\n- Daniel Defoe (1724) Roxana — Courtesan's memoir with female agency and moral ambiguity, marketed as \"true account\"\n- Cervantes (1605/1615) Don Quixote — Invented the modern novel, first-person framing + third-person hybrid, presented as translation of Arabic manuscript\n- Samuel Richardson (1740) Pamela — Epistolary novel (letters, not autobiography), \"virtue rewarded\" moral framing, launched domestic fiction genre\n- Anonymous (1700s) The Adventures of a Guinea — Unique object-narrator perspective (a coin travels through society), marketed as \"true account\"\n- Anonymous (1690s-1700s) The Complete London Spy — Early expose journalism, \"true account\" of London's underworld and criminal subculture",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1873,
			"y":7056,
			"width":990,
			"height":420,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"faf3620850641360",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe is credited for the rise of realism. It was one of the first times writing shifted away from fantastical legends and epic poems. Contrary to what came before, it shifted the focus on the individual.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1943,
			"y":6276,
			"width":660,
			"height":180,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ee92cfbfbbb3a312",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"let me tell you about a human. \na white male.\nOne of those names you probably know so well from having heard it a thousand times, so you don't even notice or pause to wonder anymore.\nWho was Daniel Defoe, the author of Robinson Crusoe, by some measures the first english novel.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2043,
			"y":6016,
			"width":500,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ebe1e9430944e499",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"a lot of words for something supposed to grab attention. The clickbait of the time, very much \"true crime\". The books were sold unbound and the binding chosen separately, so I suppose this was the cover. They relied on more words than we do today.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2703,
			"y":6676,
			"width":680,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"18253c3af95b33a7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Based on what I know:\n- Lloyd's of London (1686) — Edward Lloyd opened a coffee house where ship owners, merchants, and underwriters gathered to trade insurance. It became the world's insurance market — essentially a risk marketplace. Revolutionary.\n- The Bank of England (1694) — Created to fund war against France. First central bank in modern sense. Issued paper money, managed national debt, pioneered government-backed currency.\n- The first newspapers & advertising (1660s-1700s) — The Oxford Gazette (1665) became The London Gazette. Classified ads started appearing — \"Lost: a black poodle\" etc. The first newspaper ads were for books, medicines, and services.\n- Joint-stock companies — The East India Company (1600), South Sea Company (1711) — allowed investors to buy shares in ventures. speculation frenzy, later bubble (1720 South Sea Bubble).\n- Coffee houses as information markets — Lloyd's (insurance), Jonathan's (stock trading), Lloyd's was essentially the first information marketplace where risk itself became a tradeable commodity.\n- The first shopping — Fixed-price shops replaced haggling in the 1690s-1700s. The drum and shop became standardized retail spaces. Price tags — revolutionary.\n- Credit systems — The \"book credit\" system where merchants kept accounts with each other. Bills of exchange allowed long-distance trade without moving gold.\n- The advertising breakthrough — Robinson Crusoe's \"Written by Himself\" was part of a broader shift: selling experience rather than product. The same era saw traveling showmen, quack medicines with fake testimonials, and the birth of brand as promise.\nThe pattern: turning information into commodity, risk into market, experience into product. That's what Defoe did with Selkirk's story — and what the whole merchant class was doing with everything else.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2883,
			"y":7336,
			"width":500,
			"height":400,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"2948f28df627bb06",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was first published on  25 April 1719. Its original title was \"The Life and Strange Surprizing Adventures of Robinson Crusoe, of York, Mariner: Who lived Eight and Twenty Years, all alone in an un-inhabited Island on the Coast of America, near the Mouth of the Great River of Oroonoque; Having been cast on Shore by Shipwreck, wherein all the Men perished but himself. With An Account how he was at last as strangely deliver'd by Pyrates. Written by Himself._\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2003,
			"y":6536,
			"width":540,
			"height":240,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"8bec1a05410eb265",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"the war between words and images ??",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3383,
			"y":7026,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"e100bf5300059e3b","type":"text","text":"That's a very long title...\n---","x":-247,"y":7266,"width":390,"height":105,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"bcc0cef568d3e4b0","type":"text","text":"Wait... written by himself?\n---\n\nhistory of autobiographical fiction\nreview the notes","x":193,"y":7371,"width":300,"height":355,"color":"1"},
		{
			"id":"342b7f96d549b6be",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"this guy points out that realism and authenticity sells",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-35,
			"y":7688,
			"width":260,
			"height":120
		},
		{"id":"f57c4cd6b2dbf64e","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oeYa_Pcg4hg&t=197s","x":-607,"y":7796,"width":640,"height":360},
		{
			"id":"51d8ca31ceeb8f55",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Friday?\n---\nthe archetypal wilson\nYou know, Friday, the cannibal from the savage tribe on Robinson's island.\nRobinson tames him with civilization and they become friends.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":143,
			"y":7896,
			"width":500,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"7a2224e261f7080c","type":"file","file":"projects/ongoing/buttons/Pasted image 20260430060905.png","x":-588,"y":8225,"width":400,"height":211},
		{"id":"e8a4775851bc5327","type":"text","text":"📷 That's not the title of the book, though...\n---\nIts original title was:\n\"The\nLIFE\nand\nStrange Surprizing \nADVENTURES\nof \nRobinson Crusoe\nof York, Mariner:\n\nWho lived Eight and Twenty Years, all alone in an un-inhabited Island on the Coast of America, near the Mouth of the Great River of Oroonoque;\nHaving been cast on Shore by Shipwreck, wherein all the Men perished but himself. \n\nWITH\nAn Account how he was at last as strangely deliver'd by Pyrates. \nWritten by Himself.\"\n\nYou can see it for yourself on <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Robinson_Crusoe_1719_1st_edition.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wikimedia.\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Robinson_Crusoe_1719_1st_edition.jpg\" /></a>","x":-807,"y":5418,"width":440,"height":460,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"d59c4d2f4f4674df","type":"text","text":"Strange surprizing adventures...\n---\nLike the one you're living right now!","x":-227,"y":5948,"width":260,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{
			"id":"6f30d97bbab96763",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of book are we talking about here?\n---\nHe studied the Great Atlases and the journals of merchant travelers of the times.\nHe kept up to date with scientific colonial exploration.\nHe also had an interest for practical knowledge manuals, which would later give his own writing some realism and depth.\nSome names come to mind...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8951,
			"y":-4663,
			"width":420,
			"height":330
		},
		{
			"id":"458077cf65aea0f1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He loved to gather information.\n---\nThat was the key to a good deal.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8926,
			"y":-4275,
			"width":410,
			"height":120,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"c3f78645024410ce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I also think it's important to understand the world...\n---\nAnd where things come from.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8926,
			"y":-4103,
			"width":420,
			"height":145,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"82457e74613347a6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚢 Travel, you say?\n---\nThere is no historical record of Daniel Defoe ever setting foot outside the British Isles.\nThere are however a lot of mentions of him being an avid armchair traveler.\nHe would read everything he came across when it came to international travel.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8886,
			"y":-5083,
			"width":300,
			"height":320,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"3c15c4f2632c1045",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So... the author of Robinson Crusoe had no actual travel experience?\n---\nWell, it would appear so.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8461,
			"y":-4705,
			"width":260,
			"height":185,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"c06caf1ce1c855b9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wait, how did he reconcile that with the fact that he literaly wrote fiction?\n---\nDefoe didn’t just ignore the contradiction; he built an entire philosophical defense for it. He called his method \"Religious Allegory\" or \"Parabolic Writing.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8416,
			"y":-4088,
			"width":520,
			"height":210,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"6a3eb1ddad2c0ac6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Did Daniel Defoe read any fiction?\n---\nHe was famously suspicious of fiction, that his religious upbringing pushed him to consider \"lies told for pleasure\".\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8436,
			"y":-4323,
			"width":540,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"8676648ae26bcd81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💼 During his early life, Daniel Defoe was a travelling salesman.\n---\nHe rode thousands of miles all across England and Scotland.\nHe dealt in stockings (wool, silk, and cotton), but also branched out into wine, spirits, and tobacco.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8290,
			"y":-5188,
			"width":470,
			"height":210,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"041706c4eac2b33b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Very much a rebel entrepreneur of the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8080,
			"y":-4790,
			"width":260,
			"height":55
		},
		{
			"id":"27a1954a4a5e8183",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"With deep ties to politics...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8026,
			"y":-4642,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a331d00687a688d3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Though the rest of his life would be full of political intrigue...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7280,
			"y":-4753,
			"width":420,
			"height":110,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"bda37b01f524fff7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Glorious?\n---\nHistory is written by the winners.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7043,
			"y":-3601,
			"width":260,
			"height":120,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"f9a85ef1f4484755",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of political intrigue?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6928,
			"y":-4521,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"19a8c144da17f00b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Untill the Glorious Revolution of 1688\n---\nA group of protestant elites worried about the catholic king organized a rebellion.\nIt worked and the king's son-in-law, a protestant, acceded to the throne.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6880,
			"y":-4000,
			"width":500,
			"height":201,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"db9af5deecdd4403",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wow, what happened to him then?\n---\nDaniel Defoe received a pardon and continued business as usual.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6740,
			"y":-4197,
			"width":360,
			"height":161
		},
		{
			"id":"939fb96ab11834bf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The new king was obviously quite controversial.\n---\nDaniel Defoe defended him in a satyrical poem.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6568,
			"y":-3760,
			"width":505,
			"height":134,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"4a3fa30454592086",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"At 25, Daniel Defoe took part in the Monmouth Rebellion\n---\nA protestant failed rebellion against the catholic king.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6510,
			"y":-4559,
			"width":260,
			"height":260,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"d1a142f0a782dd56",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Monmouth Rebellion is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6263,
			"y":-4117,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"833cbd5359bf31db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The king was quite pleased with the way Daniel Defoe defended him\n---\nDaniel Defoe became somewhat of an advisor to him in the last years of his life.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6567,
			"y":-3560,
			"width":375,
			"height":224,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"892f81aa580d4be9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Glorious Revolution is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7043,
			"y":-3448,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"4ee260ef31d9900b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"\"France?\" What does France have to do with it?\n---\nDefoe was a fervent admirer of Louis XIV's militaristic centralism.\nBy talking about France, he could criticize the English government with a veneer of deniability.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5570,
			"y":-2484,
			"width":464,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"10818a63bbc83eb9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Prison?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6265,
			"y":-2324,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"be08d3a2e0fb21b3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"King William III died in 1702, his wife, Queen Anne, succeeded him to the throne.\n---\nShe was not close with Daniel Defoe. In fact she had strong ties to the Church of England.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6445,
			"y":-3305,
			"width":340,
			"height":268,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"0fa336742d48d5a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 King William III\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_III_of_England",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6743,
			"y":-3291,
			"width":225,
			"height":129
		},
		{
			"id":"d558c210ac6f1acc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 Daniel Defoe's \"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenter\"\n---\nOn the <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/shortest-way-dissenters\">Internet Archive</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/shortest-way-dissenters\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6870,
			"y":-2585,
			"width":490,
			"height":271
		},
		{
			"id":"5929c71a78c15ca1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe and his \"review\" were instrumental in the redacting of the Act of Union in 1707\n---\nYou know, the creation of a \"United Kingdom\".\nBut this is a story for another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6218,
			"y":-2550,
			"width":398,
			"height":180,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"09813e983375b960",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Queen Anne\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne,_Queen_of_Great_Britain",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6855,
			"y":-3093,
			"width":225,
			"height":129
		},
		{
			"id":"516d8b49569ae19a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenters?\n---\nIt was written from the point of view of a bloodthirsty catholic extremist.\nDaniel Defoe essentially impersonated his opponents and exaggerated their positions.\nThey initially loved it, and many believed that the government was actually planning a massacre.\nWhen the truth came out, everyone was understanbly quite upset.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7273,
			"y":-2645,
			"width":360,
			"height":390,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"a8d38e0524f7896d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of government?\n---\nThe politics at the time were an intense war between the \"progressive\" Whigs and the \"traditionalist\" Tories.\nThe balance of power was a constant tug-of-war.\nDaniel Defoe's positions were closer to the Whigs, but he was spying for the Tories.\nAs a result, he was widely distrusted and seen as a double agent.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6950,
			"y":-1963,
			"width":470,
			"height":252,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"9f04098fd76affe4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Robert Harley",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6175,
			"y":-1963,
			"width":180,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"674e6a3c3e3e61d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1703, Daniel Defoe was imprisoned for a satirical poem.\n---\nHe had published \"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenters\" anonymously but was later found out.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6825,
			"y":-2889,
			"width":400,
			"height":220,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"20daebf4571d973f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In Prison, he started his own private publication.\n---\nA newsletter if you will.\n<a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_El8TAAAAQAAJ\" target=\"_blank\">\"A Review of the Affairs of France\"</a> started as a weekly chronicle that shifted according to Daniel Defoe's interests.\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_El8TAAAAQAAJ\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5967,
			"y":-2852,
			"width":460,
			"height":267,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f2c4a78c2f9975be",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Until he became a spy for the government!\n---\nThe Tory minister Robert Harley bailed him out of prison on condition that Daniel Defoe work for him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6670,
			"y":-2237,
			"width":380,
			"height":230,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"53e72cec6b7fa897",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Spying?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6195,
			"y":-2182,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"c78c58970d985108",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Act of Union is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5737,
			"y":-2189,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"bbebbd90befb593a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He really did like allegories...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5284,
			"y":-2173,
			"width":178,
			"height":77
		},
		{
			"id":"4aa765f37fcebc89",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, Louis XIV is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5510,
			"y":-2159,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"f033b3b0f313a483",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"After the Union Act, Daniel Defoe spent a lot of time in Edinburgh spying for the government.\n---\nHe monitored political dissent on the ground.\nAs the Union settled, he turned his attention to domestic affairs.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5970,
			"y":-2026,
			"width":390,
			"height":297,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"5b9234d08f40366a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🗝️ In 1701, Daniel Defoe published The True-Born Englishman.\n---\nAt 41, he published this satirical poem against the xenophobic culture of England of the time.\nIt was aimed at defending the dutch born king, William III.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9020,
			"y":-2718,
			"width":400,
			"height":280,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"8e055b600367b97e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In essence, a pro-immigration plea?\n---\nYes,  mostly focused on calling racial purity a myth, and pointing out shameful origins of established aristocrats.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8620,
			"y":-2410,
			"width":350,
			"height":213
		},
		{
			"id":"05a22844cd0ac569",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe's True-Born Englishman was a massive hit.\n---\nFirst published anonymously, the demand for it grew quickly, and many unauthorized copies were produced.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9194,
			"y":-2254,
			"width":480,
			"height":187,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"ebf764b1093a8127",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I do like this quote:\n---\nA true-born Englishman's a contradiction,  \nIn speech an irony, in fact a fiction;",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8575,
			"y":-2162,
			"width":260,
			"height":230,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"8725a0235c1a2659",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe was quite upset that his poem had been widely copied.\n---\nIt appears that the majority of copies in circulation were cheap unauthorized versions on which he did not make any profit.\nHe claimed that up to 80000 copies were circulated, a staggering amount for the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9127,
			"y":-1831,
			"width":460,
			"height":232,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"15ffce56be6fb0f6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":" 🚰 The True-Born Englishman?\n ---\n You can read the full text on <a href=\"https://gutenberg.org/ebooks/30159\">Project Gutenberg</a>.\n \n <iframe src=\"https://gutenberg.org/ebooks/30159\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8260,
			"y":-2266,
			"width":340,
			"height":283
		},
		{
			"id":"ecbf5cec60c58bf2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The True-Born Englishman was way more successful than the original poem it was responding to.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8540,
			"y":-1845,
			"width":265,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"9cb46f16a0ebdfb7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In fact, the True-Born Englishman was a response...\n---\nIt was mostly a response to John Tutchin's 1700 poem \"The Foreigners\", attacking the Dutch ties of the king.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8215,
			"y":-1845,
			"width":360,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"1eee3e89560bf812",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 John Tutchin\n\nA radical political journalist. \nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Tutchin",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8400,
			"y":-1605,
			"width":260,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"c0925e9d6778c0a2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 John Tutchin's \"The Foreigners\"\n---\nOn the <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-foreigners-a-poem-_tutchin-john_1700/page/4/mode/2up\">Internet Archive</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-foreigners-a-poem-_tutchin-john_1700/page/4/mode/2up\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-8100,
			"y":-1605,
			"width":265,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"fd8affe066a584ff",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What happened to them after the return of the monarchy?\n---\nThey had to keep their faith secret since it was illegal.\nThousands fled to America. Most stayed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5940,
			"y":-5519,
			"width":420,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"fec336acff3f8ceb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What's a Dissenter?\n---\nAs opposed to Conformists, Dissenters are catholic protestants who refuse the English monarch as \"pope\".\nA lot of them were in some ways refugees from the continent fleeing persecution.\nSome were of French or Flemish origins.\nThey were the religious movement underpinning the republican revolution a decade earlier, and the puritan dictatorship that succeeded.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5930,
			"y":-6130,
			"width":420,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"7ed03c78122433b8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe received the closest thing to an MBA that existed in the 17th century.\n---\nMathematics and languages as business tools.\nWith a heavy dose of religious and ethical leaning.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5850,
			"y":-6550,
			"width":340,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"31793240f96b9314",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"It's called \"networking\"\n---\nThe ruling class helping their owns.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5440,
			"y":-6530,
			"width":370,
			"height":110,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"1364f5e6e7a345df",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So Daniel Defoe grew up in a cultish microcosm? \n---\nHe did live in rebel, dissentious districts. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5540,
			"y":-5219,
			"width":360,
			"height":160,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"4c31c73f1f9710ad",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wealthy bourgeois merchants, religious extremists, fallen from power...\n---\nAnd hungry for revenge?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5590,
			"y":-5725,
			"width":460,
			"height":150,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"239181232a3195da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"📍 Does the school building remain?\n---\nThe building did not survive the test of time, but \n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?client=firefox-b-d&q=Charles+Morton+Court\" target=\"_blank\">Charles Morton Court Prestige Student Housing</a> serve as a testament to its legacy.\nIn 2026, it seems to stand above a \"Papa Johns\" and a \"Tesco Express\".\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Prestige+Student+Living+Charles+Morton+Court/@51.5527125,-0.0872473,3a,75y,208.91h,112.2t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-22.199785617299767%26panoid%3DO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A%26yaw%3D208.90594074437794!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!3m5!1s0x48761c86b08c46fb:0xd1ae95b42a8e4ec0!8m2!3d51.5526405!4d-0.0873502!16s%2Fg%2F1vvr5q51?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDQyOS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\" target=\"_blank\">Here is Street View and Google Maps reviews.</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Prestige+Student+Living+Charles+Morton+Court/@51.5527125,-0.0872473,3a,75y,208.91h,112.2t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-22.199785617299767%26panoid%3DO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A%26yaw%3D208.90594074437794!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!3m5!1s0x48761c86b08c46fb:0xd1ae95b42a8e4ec0!8m2!3d51.5526405!4d-0.0873502!16s%2Fg%2F1vvr5q51?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDQyOS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6480,
			"y":-6570,
			"width":400,
			"height":440,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"473213a9261ef550",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A famous religious and intellectual microcosm for Dissenters.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6790,
			"y":-6320,
			"width":260,
			"height":190
		},
		{
			"id":"894d8e9bcd6407da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What the *** is a green?\n---\nYou know? The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_green\" target=\"_blank\">greens</a>! From the inclosures!",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6470,
			"y":-6040,
			"width":420,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4e956b014fce2d23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Inclosure story is another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6360,
			"y":-5840,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"9e51b34e90f4962c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Newington Green is a district in the North of London\n---\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newington_Green",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6820,
			"y":-7080,
			"width":430,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"9545fa71db756f1a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"They taught that the \"Flow of Goods\" was God’s way of bringing the world together.\n---\nProperty Rights and Contract Law were nearly as sacred as the Ten Commandments.\n\nThis gave Defoe a lifelong obsession with the \"Dignity of Trade.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5420,
			"y":-6210,
			"width":360,
			"height":320,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"adc2ce5c16579335",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 I hope you have a source for that\n---\nPeter Earle and Maximillian E. Novak",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5070,
			"y":-5710,
			"width":380,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"7099e851dc27ad23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of protestantism is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4044,
			"y":-5172,
			"width":260,
			"height":120,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"0a424ad760b7b70b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"✝️ The protestant work ethic?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4524,
			"y":-5502,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"531817bb5f8b90ca",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Dignity?\n---\nAs opposed to the \"decadent\" birthright aristocracy.\nToo complacent.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5340,
			"y":-5459,
			"width":320,
			"height":160,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"40eb47c3ef08be5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💵 That worldview where money is a measure of salute?\n---\nMoney really is the ultimate signifier...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4264,
			"y":-5392,
			"width":440,
			"height":140,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"61c6472d86377400",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"📷 This guy?\n---\nYou can see it for yourself on <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wikimedia.\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg/960px-Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg\" /></a>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6160,
			"y":-7892,
			"width":260,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"18924d63f3bc5fa5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💸 They made it!\n---\nThey were \"New Money\".\nThe American dream...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6360,
			"y":-7372,
			"width":260,
			"height":145,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"e1aff5886c20bec7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The school was shut down due to religious pressure when Defoe was a young adult.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6430,
			"y":-7190,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"f9a56dfc6bb04b2e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The best education money could buy?\n---\nBack in those days, the standard prestigious institutions like Oxford or Cambridge required religious oath to the English religion, which some, like Daniel Defoe's family refused to take.\n\nInstead, they formed privately financed and operated so-called \"Dissenter\" academies, private boarding school which rapidly surpassed the old establishments in the technologies they could afford.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5960,
			"y":-7190,
			"width":420,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"061ca5ebd5e9bf0f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Charles Morton\n---\nA pioneer intellectual who revolutionized education.\nHe ran a private academy for 20 years before moving to America.\nHe went on to have a major role in the founding of Harvard University.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6750,
			"y":-6840,
			"width":360,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"cc2c941236c1540a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Do I smell the distinctive odor of business school?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5520,
			"y":-6760,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{"id":"d3b4516848b37f0d","type":"text","text":"⏰ Daniel Defoe was born in 1660\n---\nIt was a time of renewal, as Charles II restored the monarchy after a brief attempt at a progressive republic that devolved into a puritan military dictatorship.","x":-7050,"y":-8040,"width":460,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{
			"id":"3df206418f34d8a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So it usually goes with revolutions...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7570,
			"y":-7840,
			"width":400,
			"height":60,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"ef60b9e37b51d7da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So far\n---\nBut people learn...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7370,
			"y":-7700,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"5b4d451a709f0da1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Daniel Defoe's family were London candle makers.\n---\nA successful bourgeois family.\nHis father was the first born in England from a puritan Flemish family fleeing religious persecution.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7060,
			"y":-7670,
			"width":480,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"21687949a41e78bc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe studied in Charles Morton’s Academy at Newington Green\n---\nIt was a brand new kind of education.\nThe best money could buy.\nIt was a very progressive education focused on knowledge for the sake of doing.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7530,
			"y":-7150,
			"width":480,
			"height":245,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"9fa40b1636a990f3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Woodes Rogers",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2812,
			"y":-4514,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"e7fb061c2668af1b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 William Dampier",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2312,
			"y":-4484,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"62d86a09b83de173",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"1697 bestseller, _A New Voyage Round the World_\n\nwilliam dampier",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2732,
			"y":-4424,
			"width":550,
			"height":200,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"dfad503cfd74c9d8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He went on to have a complicated love life and ended up joining the navy.\n---\nWhat little was left of his wealth got caught in legal domestic battles.\n\nFor the second time in his life, three decades and a whole story older, he took to the sea to run away from his troubles.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":265,
			"y":1140,
			"width":490,
			"height":280,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"c767cde4a3b190c0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/@56.2129711,-2.9355305,3a,31.2y,332.1h,104.39t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1s7tvQIzefah1q7rzl6x5u6Q!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-14.388007490191143%26panoid%3D7tvQIzefah1q7rzl6x5u6Q%26yaw%3D332.09657819260855!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!14m5!1m4!2m3!1sChdDSUhNMG9nS0VJQ0FnSURldk0tcjVRRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x22de508f2ac7ea86?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDUwNi4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2560,
			"y":1360,
			"width":380,
			"height":320,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"7c503ef2307d7244",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 2023, a google map review shares how much of a comfort this statue has been through 55 years of life...\nhttps://maps.app.goo.gl/VmUF2Xb33mxgRy6H7",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2160,
			"y":1514,
			"width":360,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"180be824b8cb2ff1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ In 1720, as Robinson Crusoe was becoming a bestseller, Alexander Selkirk quietly shipped out on the HMS Weymouth off the coast of Africa.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":265,
			"y":1460,
			"width":485,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4c90dc1074065680",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alexander Selkirk did not enjoy the fame.\n---\nHe did not like being a curiosity.\n\nHe would reportedly remark \"I am now worth eight hundred pounds, but shall never be as happy as when I was not worth a farthing\".",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":260,
			"y":840,
			"width":500,
			"height":240,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"e693ab12b82102e0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Miskito history is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2020,
			"y":-240,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"38ce23dbc4abf051",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Robin",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":590,
			"y":-173,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"7861245cc5620df9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Thomas Roscoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1560,
			"y":40,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"8503bbea5714258d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 Alexander Selkirk did not survive the outbreak of yellow fever that broke through the ship.\n---\nHe did not have children.\nIn 1885, a self-made net manufacturer, descendant of his brother erected a bronze statue to his memory in his hometown.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2400,
			"y":1020,
			"width":490,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"26cf16be5a4e1cdc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Voyage_to_the_South_Sea,_and_Round_the_World",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":820,
			"y":650,
			"width":420,
			"height":190
		},
		{
			"id":"4002dae8248d4756",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Edward Cooke",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":790,
			"y":920,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f1f51c829d144f82",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/55538",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":180,
			"y":644,
			"width":330,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"178ee6244499988f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He hid from Spanish sailors who stopped to pee under the very tree he was hiding in.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":280,
			"y":-700,
			"width":260,
			"height":135
		},
		{
			"id":"bd486b17b7b63c96",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He worried that he would die like this.\n---\nAnd that his feline companions would end up eating him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":560,
			"y":-600,
			"width":260,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"d735c08134aa72b0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will_(Indian)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":690,
			"y":-860,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"bb96389d71c92713",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Lower Largo, Fife, Scotland, ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":660,
			"y":-2969,
			"width":260,
			"height":147,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"0960ba5e853c2d1b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ The life of Alexander Selkirk\n---\nAlexander Selkirk was born in 1676 in a small fishing village of Scotland.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":290,
			"y":-3180,
			"width":410,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"fbd28305ec6e9c0f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He was quite a rebellious and unruly child.\n---\nHe was the seventh son of a shoemaker, which promised him a great destiny according to a folk superstition.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-70,
			"y":-2997,
			"width":410,
			"height":175,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"a509f01839405734",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"At 17, in trouble for his behavior at church, he ran away to become a Buccaneer.\n---\nIt was a common way to make a quick buck.\nGoing back and forth to the Caribbean took around a year and a third of the crew often died in the process.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-70,
			"y":-2708,
			"width":430,
			"height":228,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"91107c03708a6f6e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1703, he joined the expedition of William Dampier.\n---\nTwo ships set sail from Ireland on September 11, in search of enemy ships to destroy and loot.\n\nSelkirk was one of the navigators of the companion ship, having learnt the in-demand skills of mathematics and astronomy in his past experience.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":163,
			"y":-2380,
			"width":435,
			"height":300,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"bbaa360c7384971c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The two ships ended up separating, and Alexander Selkirk went south with Captain Thomas Stradling, all the way to the coast of Chile.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":181,
			"y":-2040,
			"width":400,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"bbc20a2e28182560",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ On 2 February 1709\n---\nThe shipwrecked Alexander Selkirk was rescued by Captain Woodes Rogers.\nAlexander was described to be in surprisingly good state.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-220,
			"y":-780,
			"width":330,
			"height":260,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"33ce6262cbd1326f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"As they stopped to refill on a small deserted island, Alexander Selkirk had a fight with Captain Thomas Stradling.\n---\nAlexander wanted to do maintenance on the ship. \nThomas said it wasn't necessary.\nAlexander said he'd rather stay alone on the island than risking sailing off in this state.\nThomas took him at his word.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":181,
			"y":-1879,
			"width":400,
			"height":359,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"1ae63739b63c26cf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Isla+Robinson+Crusoe/@-33.6397812,-78.8460569,3a,15y,318.42h,77.29t/data=!3m8!1e1!3m6!1sCIHM0ogKEICAgIDT-euCKg!2e10!3e11!6shttps:%2F%2Flh3.googleusercontent.com%2Fgpms-cs-s%2FABJJf51T0ZaeyCLM1mCdbw4Tca4f3K097Jn4nuPHQRGn60Wk89Y6CDWsrCXdPO0_NHN8Tg_FcykUqC_lZGGWPge_V1WWX7i-0WSCr_hK9n-Cqmi78S64eLk2dWsvvFQBQKPIRZddq681%3Dw900-h600-k-no-pi12.71353919612109-ya318.42379835851926-ro0-fo100!7i8192!8i4096!4m12!1m5!3m4!2zMzPCsDM4JzI5LjAiUyA3OMKwNTAnMjguMCJX!8m2!3d-33.6413889!4d-78.8411111!3m5!1s0x96ff956094057d0d:0x7fd95ad2e2c9752!8m2!3d-33.6738424!4d-78.9363924!16s%2Fg%2F11j0h_t07r?hl=en&entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDUwNi4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":-1534,
			"width":380,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"ea18f1cafc674a0c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The ship sank within days.\n---\nSome of the crew survived and was imprisoned.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-180,
			"y":-1379,
			"width":260,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"f669887e913cc68b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 20 years earlier, a pirate named Will was abandoned on this island.\n---\nHe was too slow when his crew ran away from Spanish soldiers.\n\nHe would survive a few years on his own, before being rescued by William Dampier in 1684.\n\nNot much is known about him, apart from a brief mention in Dampier's journals.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":820,
			"y":-1363,
			"width":440,
			"height":298,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"4985caa70efc35f7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alexander Selkirk would have to survive all alone for 4 years.\n---\nHe befriended a population of feral cats that would keep him company and keep rodents away from his supplies.\nHe had to hide from passing enemy vessels while hoping for rescue.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":190,
			"y":-1335,
			"width":420,
			"height":270,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"f2bca80414c0463c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"When Dampier's ship approached Will, a Miskito crew member named Robin was the first to greet him, in a ritualistic embrace.\n---\nIt is suspected to be the origin for the name Robinson Crusoe.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":840,
			"y":-752,
			"width":420,
			"height":312,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"d0280adeca4940b9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Will was Miskito.\n---\nThe Miskito were an indigenous people from Central America who allied with the British against the Spanish colonial settlers.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1370,
			"y":-756,
			"width":450,
			"height":173
		},
		{
			"id":"b08f9a775e13eb15",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Juan Fernandez island, also known as Más a Tierra, would be officially renamed Robinson Crusoe Island in 1966\n---\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinson_Crusoe_Island",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1700,
			"y":-1177,
			"width":360,
			"height":255
		},
		{
			"id":"10c2d3659f2925de",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Miskito population is now split between Nicaragua and Honduras, mostly in North Caribbean Coast Autonomous Region.\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Caribbean_Coast_Autonomous_Region",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1840,
			"y":-560,
			"width":500,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"8d6b75f1d725745d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1831, Thomas Roscoe was one of the first person to connect the story of this Will from Dampier's journals to the character of Friday in Robinson Crusoe.\n---\nIt is a common understanding that Will is the inspiration from the character of Friday, despite one being shipwrecked and the other a native.\n\nIt should be stressed that the Miskito have never had anything to do with cannibalism, and a great deal of racist conflations were done by everyone involved.\n\nIt is not known to what measure Daniel Defoe was writing out of racist ignorance, out of deliberate sensationalist marketing, or out of propagandist political agenda.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1300,
			"y":-520,
			"width":470,
			"height":480,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"26d00467d9154085",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 Fictional happy ending\n---\nSince there are no historical accounts of their lives, at least we can imagine Will and Robin happy together at sea, dying of scurvy while trying to push colonial settlers away from their homeland.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":860,
			"y":-346,
			"width":380,
			"height":213,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"0fe62e120bfb246d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alexander Selkirk looted a few riches as a navigator buccaneer, before returning to England in 1711.\n---\nHis shares of the plundered wealth appeared to amount to £800.\nBut it is his story that really captured attention.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1,
			"y":-200,
			"width":440,
			"height":313,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"52d13464d929336f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Within a year, Edward Cooke and Woodes Rogers had both published their account of the story.\n---",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":360,
			"y":340,
			"width":460,
			"height":240,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"62f48efa23a1d79f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pjfYGsKJ-Qg",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":460,
			"y":-360,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"28d8ce86f06d2354",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.williamkinnaird.com/blog/history/alexander-selkirk-the-real-robinson-crusoe/#:~:text=There%20was%20an%20old%20superstition,be%20destined%20for%20great%20things.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":-2751,
			"width":260,
			"height":172
		},
		{
			"id":"1410edb0001a9903",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Scotland was an independent nation.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":630,
			"y":-2822,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"2bc2096f54323be2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What's a buccanneer?\n---\nBuccaneers were mercenaries who raided Spanish ships on semi-official behalf of the English crown.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-960,
			"y":-2296,
			"width":460,
			"height":140,
			"color":"1"
		}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"0e839c2e-8cd3-4de9-ba1d-b1e5b222b8c8","fromNode":"title","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"title_aside","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"dae756de-8db4-429b-a745-8febe1e91f61","fromNode":"title_aside","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"title_aside2","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"2687df4f-7479-4db4-9b50-402fb684edaa","fromNode":"title_aside","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"49209c0bf5486f14","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"d64567f7-0f5c-4ab6-9baf-e8ff69d09b3c","fromNode":"d0c1f86e10f949da","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"f919133328a5e0d3","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"8bd4bb08-3d4a-41f3-8242-cdb334466b18","fromFloating":false,"fromNode":"d0c1f86e10f949da","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d674c3e21ca88716","toSide":"top","color":"4"},
		{"id":"9e0d1bf4-2845-4e75-83d0-6c146e8ee904","fromNode":"d0c1f86e10f949da","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"52cd36ea8d4ef506","fromFloating":false,"fromNode":"d674c3e21ca88716","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d3b4516848b37f0d","toSide":"top","color":"4"},
		{"id":"f06bffbc-d1fe-46e1-a6de-df010c028da7","toFloating":false,"fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"e100bf5300059e3b","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"aa5f385a-3d84-4748-8616-bec943d8aae7","fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"bcc0cef568d3e4b0","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"7045d400115a7f8e","fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"d59c4d2f4f4674df","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"12c103b1ee1807ae","toFloating":false,"fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"6fc3fd90e4ff2bf8","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"62b7eb7bf325f983","fromNode":"e8a4775851bc5327","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"72d5074d19b63713","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"8dd48722b8762bed","fromNode":"72d5074d19b63713","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"ed26200129d229b8","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"679b79ddacbb271e","fromNode":"72d5074d19b63713","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"99677d0088771e0d","toSide":"top"},
		{
			"id":"88b263b016db99dd",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"toFloating":false,
